from pathlib import Path
exec(Path('verification/state-guides/research/build-b.py').read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig').split("add('Maine'")[0])
add('Montana','MT','Montana bookkeeping for resale documentation, specialized taxes, annual payroll reconciliation and property record preservation.','Montana has no general sales tax, but resale paperwork, specialized taxes and employer accounts still require organized evidence.',[
('General Sales Tax And Resale Purchases',[1],['Montana does not have a general sales tax. Its Business Registry Resale Certificate can be provided to an outside vendor, but the vendor is not required to accept it and another state may require its own form. Preserve the certificate supplied and the delivery facts with the purchase.','A Montana address does not resolve taxes on transactions delivered or performed elsewhere. As a bookkeeping practice, retain customer destinations, travel and inventory locations for multistate review. Assess specialized activities such as lodging and resort operations with the appropriate professional before deciding which accounts are needed.']),
('Annual Withholding Reconciliation',[2,3],['Montana requires a withholding account for businesses issuing Montana wages or specified wage and withholding statements. Every year an account remains open, an annual MW-3 reconciliation is required. The agency specifies wage statements for Montana wages even where no tax was withheld.','Keep worker instructions, payroll registers, payments and statement submission evidence. Reconcile the annual filing with the wage ledger before sending it to the preparer. Maintain unemployment and insurance records separately from income withholding, and preserve closure evidence if the last payroll ends an account.']),
('Vendor And Income Evidence',[],['An outfitting business may buy resale merchandise, rental equipment and supplies for its own use from the same supplier. Describe each item and its purpose in the vendor record. Match the invoice with receiving evidence, any resale certificate and payment; preserve credit memoranda for returned stock.','Separate customer booking deposits from earned receipts under the agreed accounting basis. Keep borrowing, owner advances and refund transactions identifiable in the bank reconciliation. An equipment purchase needs acquisition and financing records so the preparer can determine basis and debt treatment.']),
('Monthly Statements And Seasonal Cash',[],['Review bank balances, cards, booking deposits, supplier bills and equipment debt before preparing monthly statements. A seasonal business should show the amount of cash committed to future bookings and payroll alongside the profit and loss statement. Explain cancellations and refunds with their original reservation references.','Keep a calendar of the actual employer and specialized tax accounts assigned to the business. Match payments with the appropriate liability account and investigate notices before closing the month. A zero sales tax account is not evidence that every other state or local obligation has been evaluated.']),
('Hypothetical Resale Order',[1],['Hypothetical example: a Montana outdoor shop orders merchandise from a Washington supplier. The supplier requests Washington documentation instead of accepting the Montana resale certificate. The bookkeeper saves the request, shipment destination and completed paperwork, then refers unresolved treatment to the preparer. The scenario does not promise that the Montana form creates an exemption elsewhere.']),
('Year End And Retention',[4],['Provide the preparer with annual sales and booking reconciliations, vendor totals, equipment additions and disposals, debts, MW-3 support and unresolved specialized tax questions. Preserve the descriptions behind resale stock and rental equipment classifications.','Montana DOR recommends keeping ordinary tax records through the limitation period, usually three years after the return was due or filed, whichever is later. Its property guidance calls for records during ownership and at least five years after reporting disposition; loss records should remain through the claim years and at least five years after the final claim. These income and property recommendations do not establish an employer record destruction schedule.'])],[('Montana Sales Tax Guidance','https://revenue.mt.gov/taxes/general-sales-tax',['No general sales tax and limits on outside vendor acceptance of Montana resale certificate.']),('Montana Withholding Accounts','https://revenue.mt.gov/taxes/withholding-tax/accounts',['Account opening and closing requirements.']),('Montana Wage Withholding Returns And Payments','https://revenue.mt.gov/taxes/withholding-tax/wage-withholding-returns-and-payments',['Annual MW-3 for open accounts and required Montana wage statements.']),('Montana Tax Record Preservation','https://revenue.mt.gov/taxes/tax-records',['Ordinary tax limitation period, extended cases, property and loss preservation recommendations.'])],['Current employer withholding and unemployment retention must be independently confirmed. Proposed rule text was excluded as proof of a current rule.'])
add('Nebraska','NE','Nebraska bookkeeping for delivery records, purchase use tax, separate state wages and retention tied to filed returns.','Nebraska reporting needs delivery evidence for sales tax and separately identified Nebraska wages in payroll records.',[
('Sales, Delivery And Purchase Use Tax',[1],['Nebraska sales regulations generally locate a sale where the purchaser takes delivery. Keep the actual receiving address and item description with each invoice. The record rules require books, supporting documents, return workpapers and exemption evidence, including support for deductions from gross receipts.','In payables, retain invoices showing where goods or services are used and the sales tax already paid. Route purchases without tax to a use tax review schedule. Document the preparer decision and any credit for tax properly paid elsewhere rather than assuming an outside supplier means the purchase is exempt.']),
('Payroll Records And Employee Statements',[2],['Nebraska withholding rules call for Nebraska wages and withholding to be stated separately from amounts for other jurisdictions on payroll records furnished to employees. Preserve work allocation information, wage registers and employee instructions so the payroll professional can support the separation.','Reconcile the annual withholding submission with periodic filings, wage statements and bank payments. Save complete, legible copies and submission confirmations. Keep unemployment obligations distinct from income withholding, and investigate payroll provider adjustments against the original pay period.']),
('Income, Inventory And Supplier Documents',[3],['Nebraska income rules require records sufficient to establish gross income, deductions and credits. A farm related business should preserve beginning stock, purchases, sales and count adjustments alongside the general ledger. Keep any claim for a special treatment attached to the actual item and business use.','As a practical control, identify financing receipts and owner funds separately from operating revenue. Vendor bills should connect to purchase approval, receiving evidence and payment. Track asset acquisitions and improvements independently from routine repairs for the tax preparer decision.']),
('Monthly Reporting And Use Tax Review',[],['A monthly close should reconcile cash, credit cards, customer balances, unpaid bills and debt before presenting financial statements. Review deposits for future deliveries and unrecorded supplier bills with the owner. Explain material seasonality in the income statement and cash needs.','Keep a purchase tax review register with vendor, use location, amount, tax already paid and decision. Compare the completed tax workpapers with ledger liabilities and filing acknowledgments. An unresolved purchase should remain visible even when the bank reconciles.']),
('Hypothetical Delivery Change',[1],['Hypothetical example: a Nebraska equipment dealer accepts an order for customer pickup, but later delivers it to a different Nebraska location. The bookkeeper retains the changed order and proof of delivery and asks the preparer to review the tax sourcing before final reporting. A billing address alone is not substituted for the actual transaction facts.']),
('Year End And Distinct Retention Rules',[1,2,3],['Prepare the handoff with sales by delivery location, exemption support, purchase tax decisions, inventory adjustments, assets, loans and payroll reconciliations. Include open deficiency notices and unresolved credits so the preparer knows which periods remain unsettled.','Sales rule 008.07 requires at least three years after filing and preservation while relevant refund or redetermination matters remain pending. Withholding rule 013.05 requires employer filed forms and employee documents for at least three years, with unresolved notices extending preservation. Income records remain needed while material to tax administration and unresolved deficiencies. These rules do not authorize destroying property basis evidence merely because three years passed.'])],[('Nebraska Chapter 1 Sales And Use Tax','https://revenue.nebraska.gov/about/chapter-1-sales-and-use-tax',['Delivery sourcing, purchase use tax, required records and three years after filing with pending matter exceptions.']),('Nebraska Chapter 21 Withholding','https://revenue.nebraska.gov/about/chapter-21-income-tax-withholding',['Separate Nebraska wage figures and employer forms retention with unresolved notice exception.']),('Nebraska Chapter 22 Income Tax','https://revenue.nebraska.gov/about/legal-information/regulations/chapter-22-individual-income-tax',['Income evidence and records needed while material or deficiency remains unresolved.'])],['Industry exemptions, workforce retention and local rate conclusions require review; no numeric rate or deadline asserted.'])
add('Nevada','NV','Nevada bookkeeping for county sales records, purchase use tax, Modified Business Tax payroll and Commerce Tax evidence.','Nevada combines sales and use reporting with a payroll based Modified Business Tax and a separate gross revenue Commerce Tax review.',[
('Sales And County Purchase Records',[1],['Nevada sales and use taxes cover taxable tangible property transactions. Consumer use tax complements sales tax when taxable property is used, stored or consumed in Nevada without the applicable sales tax paid. Keep the invoice description and use or delivery county for review.','Link sales, returns, resale support and purchase invoices with the reporting period. A vendor bill lacking tax should enter the purchase review register. Preserve equipment delivery evidence and separate items bought for resale from items retained for company use.']),
('Modified Business Tax And Employer Wages',[2],['Nevada Modified Business Tax is based on payroll, with different employer categories and exceptions. The agency ties gross wages to the quarterly wages reported to the Employment Security Division and identifies eligible employer paid health benefits in the calculation. Preserve the wage comparison and benefit evidence.','Reconcile gross wages, employee deductions and employer costs before reviewing the MBT return. Keep unemployment reporting and MBT schedules separately identifiable even when they use the same underlying wage register. Do not assume a tax account registration creates every other required account.']),
('Commerce Tax And Revenue Classification',[3],['Nevada Commerce Tax uses Nevada gross revenue and industry classification, with an annual filing threshold. Retain revenue by business activity and the evidence for where receipts are assigned. Have the preparer evaluate the current threshold and exclusions using the actual entity facts.','For management accounting, customer deposits, operating receipts, borrowings and owner contributions should remain distinct. Accounts payable should preserve the vendor invoice, business purpose, payment and any capitalization decision. An expense recognized in the profit and loss statement does not automatically reduce a gross revenue tax base.']),
('Monthly Reports And Tax Bridges',[],['Reconcile bank accounts, card settlements, supplier balances and equipment debt before issuing monthly statements. Keep county sales totals linked to gross receipts and tax liabilities, with processing fees shown separately. Explain outstanding customer deposits and inventory differences beside the balance sheet.','Maintain a quarterly wage bridge for MBT and unemployment, plus a separate annual revenue bridge for Commerce Tax review. This allows the owner to understand why a profitable month, a large payroll and a gross revenue threshold measure different things. Keep preparer adjustments documented by period.']),
('Hypothetical Health Benefit Adjustment',[2],['Hypothetical example: a Nevada shop pays a health insurance invoice covering several employees and later receives a credit. The bookkeeper saves the coverage details, payment and credit, then gives payroll specialists the facts needed to evaluate the MBT health benefit treatment. The example assumes neither deductibility nor a specific tax saving.']),
('Year End And Preservation',[4],['At year end, supply reconciled statements, sales and use workpapers, wage comparisons, benefit support, industry revenue detail, asset records and all relevant agency acknowledgments. Keep tax account notices with their original periods and unresolved credits on a separate schedule.','Nevada DOR audit guidance states a four year minimum for registered business records and eight years for an unregistered business in the sales and use context. Preserve invoices, return workpapers, exemption documents and system exports accordingly, with longer holds where required. The usual audit lookback is not the same as the preservation minimum, and other tax or employment categories need separate review.'])],[('Nevada Sales And Use Tax','https://tax.nv.gov/tax-types/sales-tax-use-tax/',['Sales and use tax on tangible property, consumer use tax and county reporting.']),('Nevada Modified Business Tax','https://tax.nv.gov/tax-types/modified-business-tax/',['Payroll basis, wage comparison, employer categories and health benefits.']),('Nevada Commerce Tax','https://tax.nv.gov/tax-types/commerce-tax/',['Nevada gross revenue, industry categories and annual threshold.']),('Nevada Audit FAQ','https://tax.nv.gov/faqs/audits/',['Four year registered and eight year unregistered records preservation and audit record scope.'])],['Commerce Tax credit, MBT deductions, thresholds, worker status and non sales retention require specialist determination.'])
add('New Hampshire','NH','New Hampshire bookkeeping for business profits and enterprise taxes, hospitality records, employer evidence and retention.','New Hampshire has no general sales tax, but business profits, enterprise value and hospitality activity need distinct accounting support.',[
('General Sales And Hospitality Activity',[1,2],['New Hampshire has no general sales tax on goods purchased in the state. That statement does not eliminate the separate Meals and Rentals tax or another state tax where a transaction reaches another jurisdiction. Retain sales destinations and the exact activity description for professional review.','A lodging or food business should preserve room, meal, rental and refund detail separately from other receipts. Save vendor invoices and receiving evidence for all purchases. A New Hampshire supplier address should not be used to decide an outside jurisdiction obligation without the actual delivery and use facts.']),
('Business Profits And Enterprise Value',[3],['New Hampshire Business Profits Tax and Business Enterprise Tax measure different bases. The agency describes enterprise value using compensation, interest and dividends, while business profits concerns taxable business profits. Maintain separate schedules so a preparer can evaluate applicability and available credits.','As a bookkeeping practice, distinguish operating expense, debt interest, owner distributions and compensation in the ledger. A company showing a small accounting profit can still need enterprise tax review. Retain the tax professional calculation and reconciliation rather than posting an unexplained annual tax adjustment.']),
('Employer And Vendor Records',[4,5],['New Hampshire business record rules include payroll, paid and unpaid vendor invoices, bank records and contracts. State labor guidance identifies unemployment coverage as a separate employer question. Maintain wage registers, approved hours, work locations and worker agreements so payroll specialists can assess the actual relationships.','Reconcile payroll deductions and employer charges to payments and reporting confirmations. For accounts payable, link supplier bills to services or goods received, approvals and credits. A contractor invoice should describe the engagement, but its label does not by itself establish independent contractor status.']),
('Monthly Financial Statements',[],['For a hospitality business, compare booking system activity with deposits, refunds and cash settlements before preparing financial statements. Review advance bookings and amounts still owed to suppliers. Explain why current cash can include payments for services scheduled in a later period.','Maintain a monthly bridge from the ledger to the business profits and enterprise value schedules. Preserve separate information on compensation, financing costs and distributions so annual tax work can use the books directly. Review bank reconciliations, receivables, payables and debt with the owner.']),
('Hypothetical Workshop And Guest Rooms',[2,3],['Hypothetical example: a New Hampshire woodworking business adds guest rooms above its studio. The bookkeeper creates distinct receipt categories for merchandise, room stays and customer deposits, preserves booking and refund records and asks the preparer to review hospitality tax registration. The general absence of sales tax is not treated as an exemption for room receipts.']),
('Year End And Retention',[2,4],['Give the preparer annual statements, compensation and interest schedules, distribution records, hospitality reports, assets, loans and outstanding notices. Preserve filing versions and any credit carryforward calculations so next year begins with supported balances.','Rev 308.01 requires business profits records for at least three years from filing or through completion of audits, administrative appeals and judicial proceedings. Meals and Rentals operator records have a separate three year rule under RSA 78-A:19. Employer unemployment, federal tax, property basis and unresolved claims can need other periods, so do not apply the business profits minimum to every file.'])],[('New Hampshire Sales Tax FAQ','https://www.revenue.nh.gov/faq/does-new-hampshire-have-sales-tax',['No general sales tax on goods.']),('New Hampshire RSA 78-A:19','https://www.gc.nh.gov/rsa/html/V/78-A/78-A-19.htm',['Meals and Rentals operator books and three year preservation.']),('New Hampshire Business Taxes','https://www.revenue.nh.gov/taxes-glance/business-taxes',['Distinct Business Profits and Business Enterprise bases and credit relationship.']),('New Hampshire Rev 300','https://www.gc.nh.gov/rules/state_agencies/rev300.html',['Current 2025 adopted business record scope and three years from filing with proceedings exception.']),('New Hampshire Labor Worker Classification','https://www.nh.gov/labor/documents/misclassification.pdf',['Unemployment coverage and worker classification facts.'])],['Employer unemployment retention and hospitality classification need separate review. Current Rev 300 text was checked via indexed official excerpts after direct retrieval failed.'])
